<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Smith (missionary)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as the Demerara Martyr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Smith was an English missionary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> who served enslaved people in Demerara from 1817. After the Demerara rebellion of 1823 he was accused of encouraging the rising, court-martialled and sentenced to death, and died in prison in 1824 before a royal reprieve arrived. His death, as the Demerara Martyr, strengthened the abolitionist cause in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-smith-missionary.jpg" alt="John Smith (missionary)" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TimBray · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Smith,_Missionary_to_Demerara,_friend_of_slaves.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
